35
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:34,300
Thank you all.

36
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:36,080
Thanks everyone who makes the city go round.

37
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:36,840
Thanks.

38
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:43,100
My name is Clayton Shelvin.

39
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:45,400
I am the executive director here at the Freight.

40
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:53,840
And if you don't know about the freight, it is the venue you are in, in case you didn't

41
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:59,880
know, you just showed up at 2020 Addison at the heart of downtown, our arts district.

42
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:03,640
And welcome to our amazing venue.

43
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,800
We are on the verge of turning 60.

44
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,880
So we are going to be celebrating for the next two years.

45
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:14,900
And I'm really happy that we can once again host the Mayor's Address.

46
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:20,900
It is an honor to speak to a community that understands something fundamental about cities.

47
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:28,140
A city is not simply a collection of buildings, streets, businesses, and government institutions.

48
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:34,820
A city is a collection of people and the culture those people create together.

49
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:39,620
And I believe that the greatest cities in the world are great because of the people

50
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:46,520
who live in them understand that they have a role in building culture. They

51
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:51,500
don't simply consume culture, they create it. They support it, they argue about it,

52
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:58,380
they challenge it, they preserve it, and most importantly, they pass it on. That is

53
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:04,820
what makes a city feel like a place rather than just a location. And I

54
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:08,820
I believe also that art is at the center of that process of creating culture.

55
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:17,140
Art allows us to imagine something that doesn't exist yet.

56
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:21,760
It gives us language for experiences we sometimes cannot explain ourselves.

57
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:26,320
It introduces us to people whose lives may look nothing like our own.

58
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:29,740
It reminds us of where we've come from, and

59
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:32,780
it gives us permission to imagine where we might go.

60
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:36,380
That is why I believe art is not a luxury.

61
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,680
Art is a part of how humanity advances itself.

62
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:45,280
And it's a part of how communities experience a higher quality of life.

63
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:51,480
When a child discovers music, when a teenager sees themselves represented on a stage,

64
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,880
when a neighborhood gathers around a mural,

65
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:56,780
when a musician brings hundreds of people together in a room,

66
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:01,880
when an audience hears a story that changes the way they understand one another,

67
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:05,380
those aren't just peripheral experiences.

68
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:09,080
Those are the experiences through which we build empathy,

69
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:13,880
imagination, connection, identity, and community.

70
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:16,880
And that brings me to Berkeley.

71
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:21,880
I am a newer resident of Berkeley as of recent,

72
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:22,880
as of like a week.

73
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:23,880
I just moved to Berkeley.

74
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:29,880
So the old city got me after all these years.

75
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:35,720
But feeling really good about being here, and I'm really proud to play a small part

76
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:39,760
in its history through my work here at the freight and my work as a board member on the

77
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:45,580
Downtown Berkeley Association.

78
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:50,820
Berkeley has never been defined solely by its university, its politics, its neighborhoods,

79
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:53,040
or its beautiful geography.

80
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:57,500
Berkeley is defined by a creative culture that has been built by generations of people

81
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:02,320
who decided that ideas matter, that expression matters,

82
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,040
that artists matter, and that challenging conventions matter.

83
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:09,980
You can feel that culture when you walk in our streets.

84
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,700
You can hear it in our music venues.

85
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,960
You see it in our theaters, galleries, murals, festivals,

86
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:18,360
bookstores, classrooms, public spaces.

87
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:20,740
You can experience it in the artists who

88
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:23,960
choose to make their homes here and in the audiences who

89
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:26,660
continue to show up for them.

90
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:32,960
The city's own recent economic analysis recognizes arts and culture as engines of economic vitality,

91
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:40,160
while Berkeley's civic arts program continues to support artists, organizations, festivals, and public art.

92
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:42,560
Yes.

93
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:53,460
But, but, culture does not sustain itself simply because we've inherited it.

94
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:58,140
Every generation has to decide whether it will invest in the culture it wants to leave behind.

95
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:01,640
And that is the responsibility we all have today.

96
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:08,800
As executive director of the freight, I see every day what happens when people invest in a cultural institution.

97
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,820
We don't just put musicians on a stage.

98
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:18,780
Here at the freight, we create a place where people really see and meet one another.

99
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:21,640
We create opportunities for artists to make a living.

100
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:25,200
We give young people the opportunity to see themselves as creators.

101
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:27,080
We bring people downtown.

102
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:29,020
We create memories.

103
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:36,180
And we create the kind of shared experiences that make people feel that they belong to something larger than themselves.

104
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:41,900
That is what arts organizations across Berkeley do every single day.

105
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:47,300
And so the question before us is not simply how do we save an arts organization.

106
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:53,220
the larger question is what kind of city do we want Berkeley to be?

107
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:58,700
Do we want to be a city that simply remembers the creative culture that made it special?

108
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:03,300
Or do we want the city to be a city that continues to create it?

109
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:09,800
We must be willing to invest in, preserve, and build Berkeley's cultural life.

110
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:17,380
I want to acknowledge Mayor Deena Ishii for her ongoing engagement with Berkeley's cultural community.

111
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:22,000
Her office has supported initiatives, including the Berkeley Poet Laureate Program.

112
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:24,020
Yes.

113
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:29,060
And the preservation of public art.

114
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:36,640
And she recently opened her City Hall office to an artwork program celebrating Berkeley's regenerative culture.

115
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:45,340
Tonight, as we talk about the future of Berkeley, I hope we remember that culture is not something that happens somewhere else.

116
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,880
Culture happens because we make it happen.

117
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,840
It happens because artists are willing to imagine.

118
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:55,760
It happens because audiences are willing to show up in spaces like this.

119
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,080
It happens because organizations are willing to take risks.

120
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:08,000
It happens because businesses, foundations, governments, and individuals decide that creativity is something worth supporting.

121
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:15,980
And it happens because ordinary people decide that the cultural life of their city is part of their own quality of life.

122
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:20,420
The future culture of Berkeley is being built right now.

123
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:24,480
Whether you see it or not, it's being built right now.

124
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:29,980
It's being built in our theaters, our music venues, it's being built by painters, dancers,

125
00:09:29,980 --> 00:09:35,840
writers, musicians, actors, filmmakers, educators, and young people, all those people who exist

126
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,720
in our community.

127
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:42,500
And it's being built by every person who believes that a great city should be more than a place

128
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:44,400
where people live.

129
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:50,600
It should be a place where people create, connect, imagine, and belong.

130
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:52,200
That's the Berkeley I believe in.

131
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:56,040
Berkeley I believe that you all want to believe in and that's the Berkeley

132
00:09:56,040 --> 00:10:01,160
worth building together. Thank you.

133
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:19,600
And now it is my... Thank you. Thank you. It is my honor now to introduce Mayor

134
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:26,500
Adina Ishii, elected in November 2024. Adina Ishii is the first Asian American

135
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:32,280
and the first woman of color to serve as the mayor of Berkeley.

136
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:39,900
She grew up in Southern California before moving to Berkeley where she

137
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:46,340
attended Berkeley City College. We love them. And then later transferred to the

138
00:10:46,340 --> 00:10:54,020
UC Berkeley Haas School of Business. She then went on to earn a Juris Doctor from Santa Clara

139
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:59,780
University School of Law. As mayor, she is leading efforts on housing and homelessness,

140
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:04,500
both locally and regionally, serving as chair of the Alameda County Mayor's Conference Working

141
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:11,460
Group on Homelessness. She convenes the Sanctuary City Task Force monthly, and she is always excited

142
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:15,600
to continue her work improving the everyday lives of Berkelians and

143
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:23,300
carrying forward Berkeley's legacy. She currently lives in beautiful South

144
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:32,600
Berkeley with her family and three dogs. It is my distinct honor to welcome for

145
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:37,700
the second year in a row here Mayor Adina Ishii.

146
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:46,600
Thank you.

147
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:46,600


148
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:50,360
Thank you, everyone.

149
00:11:56,760 --> 00:11:57,860
Thank you.

150
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:06,240
I haven't even said anything yet.

151
00:12:06,640 --> 00:12:07,880
Oh, my gosh.

152
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:09,560
Thank you all so much.

153
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:11,360
Really good evening, everyone.

154
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:17,460
Thank you so much for coming to my second State of the City address here at the Freight in the heart of downtown Berkeley.

155
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:21,460
And thank you so much to the Freight for hosting us. Can we give them a round of applause please?

156
00:12:28,460 --> 00:12:40,460
All right. Last year, I made an official mayoral visit to Japan, representing the city of Berkeley as part of the Japan-US Leadership Summit.

157
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:48,140
It was a diplomatic visit, a chance to meet Mayor Hideki Nagafuji of Sakai, our Japanese

158
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:53,300
sister city, and an opportunity to develop business relationships and bring more visitors

159
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:56,240
from Japan to Berkeley.

160
00:12:56,240 --> 00:13:00,240
This experience was powerful and eye-opening.

161
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:06,040
Living in our bubble here, it's easy to forget that Berkeley has an international reputation.

162
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:10,860
Everywhere we traveled in Japan, people knew about our city.

163
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:13,560
I know our city punches above its weight.

164
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:18,260
At the same time, the enthusiasm from the people of Japan was humbling.

165
00:13:18,260 --> 00:13:22,420
I left feeling even more proud of this city and motivated to think bigger about how we

166
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:28,400
turn that international reputation into opportunity here at home for our businesses, workers,

167
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:30,720
and local economy.

168
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:34,180
People around the world know Berkeley.

169
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:39,720
They think of movements that have changed history, groundbreaking discoveries, cutting-edge

170
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:42,400
research and innovation.

171
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:46,640
And we need to make sure Berkeley is also known as a place to do business, create jobs

172
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:48,680
and grow companies.

173
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:53,500
That means supporting the businesses already here, attracting new ones, and diversifying

174
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:59,420
our economy so we're not relying on the same sources of revenue year after year.

175
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:04,320
That matters more as we think about our budget deficit.

176
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,040
We can't just keep making cuts.

177
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:11,480
We also have to look at where Berkeley can grow, whether that's by supporting new businesses

178
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:16,800
or encouraging people to visit Berkeley to eat, shop and discover.

179
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:21,660
My job as mayor is to keep that spirit alive and bring it to the challenges we face today,

180
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:26,260
from supporting our local businesses to building more housing and making Berkeley a place people

181
00:14:26,260 --> 00:14:28,660
can afford to call home.

182
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:30,660
What...

183
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:36,240
When it comes to businesses founded in Berkeley,

184
00:14:36,400 --> 00:14:39,920
I want the next great idea to not just start here, but stay here.

185
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:41,620
And that's what tonight is about.

186
00:14:42,020 --> 00:14:44,360
This is why my address is called Doing Business in Berkeley.

187
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:48,340
And do you know why people around the world know Berkeley?

188
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:53,840
It's because we're home to the number one public university in the world.

189
00:14:55,460 --> 00:14:56,260
Go Bears!

190
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:07,300
It's impossible to deny the role the university played in putting our town on the map.

191
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:12,420
Some worry that the university's success harms the city, but we're partners, and both parties

192
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:15,300
benefit from each other's success.

193
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,120
This is something that I actively work on as mayor.

194
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:26,140
I meet regularly with UC Berkeley's chancellor, Rich Lyons, and we constantly talk about

195
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:32,640
how we can strengthen our partnership. The Chancellor is an excellent collaborator, and

196
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:36,460
I can confidently say that the relationship between UC Berkeley and the City of Berkeley

197
00:15:36,460 --> 00:15:39,660
is the best it's ever been.

198
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:47,700
A perfect example of our partnership is the collaborative projects funded by the Long

199
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:52,420
Range Development Plan, which brings over $4 million into the City each year from the

200
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:57,760
University. One of these projects, the Southside Complete Streets, was recently finished. This

201
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:01,740
project transformed three streets bordering the south side of campus, making it safer

202
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:07,640
for pedestrians, bikes, and buses to move around the neighborhood.

203
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:16,700
And that spirit of partnership hasn't slowed down. Just a few months ago, I sat alongside

204
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:22,520
Chancellor Lyons as a shark tank style judge at the Civic Innovation Challenge

205
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:26,960
where Cal students had seven days to tackle problems our city faces from

206
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:31,400
disaster planning to small business support to accessibility. Watching

207
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:35,120
students develop solutions to problems the city has been wrestling with was so

208
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:40,040
inspiring. That's the kind of partnership I want more of. The talent and creativity

209
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:44,100
of Cal students and faculty working alongside the city to solve real

210
00:16:44,100 --> 00:16:50,140
problems for Berkeley. The Chancellor and I go way back. He was actually the dean of the

211
00:16:50,140 --> 00:16:55,080
hospital business when I was a student there. Who knew we would grow up to be Instagram influencers?

212
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:03,520
We're closely aligned in how we think about Berkeley as a whole, particularly when it comes

213
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:09,100
to innovation and entrepreneurship. The university is truly a hub of opportunity for our city.

214
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:15,180
How many of you knew that UC Berkeley has the most venture-backed startups of any university in the world?

215
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:16,260
Yeah.

216
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:20,480
Yeah, they have now, for four years in a row.

217
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:24,860
UC Berkeley also has the most female-founded startups of any college globally.

218
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:26,220
Yes.

219
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:29,580
Really incredible.

220
00:17:31,080 --> 00:17:35,040
Today, the city of Berkeley is home to about 370 innovation companies,

221
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:38,400
from biotech to robotics, manufacturing to life sciences,

222
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:42,340
and we have so many amazing partners that are shaping and cultivating environments

223
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:45,320
where early-stage startups can grow and thrive.

224
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:50,100
Within a few blocks of the freight are organizations that are building the future.

225
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:57,380
Berkeley Skydeck, Baker Labs, the new IGI Baker Labs building with Jennifer Doudna,

226
00:17:57,760 --> 00:17:59,440
and the Berkeley Gateway Accelerator.

227
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:06,840
The city is intentional about partnering with these institutions

228
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:08,340
through the Berkeley Startup Cluster.

229
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:13,440
A lot of fans, I like it.

230
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:17,080
Okay, this collaboration includes the city's Office of Economic Development,

231
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:22,040
UC Berkeley, Lawrence Berkeley National Lab, the Berkeley Chamber, and business districts.

232
00:18:22,580 --> 00:18:25,640
It was formed to help make Berkeley more attractive to startups,

233
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:28,180
especially those spinning out of Cal and the Lawrence Berkeley Lab.

234
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:33,140
We want those companies to see Berkeley as a place to launch, grow, and stay.

235
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:35,820
And we're already seeing that vision come to life.

236
00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:42,700
Recently, my team connected with Ambi Robotics, a company focused on tackling the supply chain's

237
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:45,620
most complex problems with AI-powered robots.

238
00:18:46,200 --> 00:18:50,440
